<header class="sp-head"><p class="featured-region">1811 · Osage country</p><h1 class="sp-title">Tecumseh to the Osages</h1><p class="sp-by"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tecumseh">Tecumseh</a></p></header>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>The Shawnee leader Tecumseh travelled widely to build a confederacy of Native nations against further American expansion. A speech he is said to have given to the Osage was recorded years later by a white man who had lived among them as a captive.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The words</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>Brothers, we all belong to one family; we are all children of the Great Spirit.</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">As recorded by John Dunn Hunter, Memoirs of a Captivity (1823); some historians doubt the account.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>Tecumseh's confederacy allied with Britain in the War of 1812, and he was killed at the Battle of the Thames in 1813. With him ended the last great effort to unite the peoples of the eastern woodlands against the United States.</p></section>
